<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sebastian of Portugal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1554">1554</a>–<a href="/years/1578">1578</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Sebastião I · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-aviz">House of Aviz</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sebastian of Portugal (Sebastião I) was King of Portugal from 1557 until 1578. He led an invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> that ended in disaster at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-alcacer-quibir">Battle of Alcácer Quibir</a> in 1578, where he was killed and his army destroyed by Saadi forces. His death without an heir led to a succession crisis and the union of the Portuguese and Spanish crowns in 1580.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sebastian-of-portugal.jpg" alt="Sebastian of Portugal" width="320" height="379" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cristóvão de Morais · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sebasti%C3%A3o_de_Portugal,_c._1571-1574_-_Crist%C3%B3v%C3%A3o_de_Morais_(Google_Art_Project).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sebastian was born on 20 January 1554, the feast of Saint Sebastian, whose name he was given. He was the only child of João Manuel, Prince of Portugal, and Joanna of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, and was born two weeks after his father's death, so he was heir apparent from birth. His mother left him soon afterwards to serve as regent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and never saw him again. He succeeded his grandfather, King John III, at the age of three, and a regency followed, first under his grandmother Catherine of Austria and then under his great-uncle Cardinal Henry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evora">Évora</a>.</p><p>He grew up under heavy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> influence and became extremely devout, carrying a copy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-aquinas">Thomas Aquinas</a> at his belt. He did not marry, and a plan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-de-medici">Catherine de' Medici</a> to marry him to her daughter Margaret of Valois failed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>During his short personal reign, which began when he attained his majority in 1568, Sebastian strengthened ties with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and restructured much of the kingdom's administrative, judicial and military life. In 1569 Duarte Nunes de Leão compiled the laws in the Código Sebastiânico, and in 1570 the king ordered that Brazilian Indians should not be used as slaves. During the plague of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> in 1569 he brought doctors from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> and created two hospitals.</p><p>Sebastian dreamed of a great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, where several Portuguese way stations on the route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> had been lost. His chance came in 1576 when Abu Abdallah Mohammed II Saadi lost his throne and fled to Portugal. In June 1578 an army of 17,000 men, including foreign <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> and almost all the country's nobility, sailed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-alcacer-quibir">Battle of Alcácer Quibir</a>, also called the Battle of the Three Kings, the Portuguese army was routed by Abd al-Malik at the head of more than 60,000 men. Sebastian was last seen riding headlong into the enemy lines. Philip II claimed to have received his remains and buried them in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeronimos-monastery">Jerónimos Monastery</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> after 1580, but the body could not be identified. His great-uncle Henry succeeded him. The belief that he would return, called Sebastianism, led to four pretenders during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberian-union">Iberian Union</a> of 1580 to 1640, and he came to be known as the Desired and the Hidden. His reputation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> remained positive as a symbol of chivalry.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sebastian, King of Portugal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Disaster came in Morocco, where King <b>Sebastian</b> died with much of the nobility at the Battle of Alcácer Quibir in 1578.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The young King <b>Sebastião I</b> led an army of some 20,000 Portuguese, foreign mercenaries and Moroccan allies against the Saadian Sultan Abd al-Malik, and it was annihilated.</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The deposed sultan Muhammad al-Mutawakkil had enlisted King <b>Sebastian of Portugal</b> against his uncle Abd al-Malik, who had taken the throne in 1576 with Ottoman help.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saadi-sultanate">Saadi Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>King Sebastian of Portugal</b> died without heirs at Alcácer Quibir in Morocco in 1578, and his successor, the elderly Cardinal Henry, died in 1580.</blockquote><a href="/state/iberian-union">Iberian Union</a></li><li><blockquote>King <b>Sebastian</b>'s disastrous invasion ended at Alcácer Quibir in 1578.</blockquote><a href="/state/portuguese-morocco">Portuguese Morocco</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ribeira Palace, in what is now <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>. Died in Ksar el-Kebir, in what is now <a href="/countries/morocco">Morocco</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.71,-9.14&amp;year=1569" aria-label="Ribeira Palace on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Sebastian of Portugal"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4393 4234h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4393 4234h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.71,-9.14&amp;year=1569">What was here in 1569? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
